  {
    "annotations": {
      "destructiveHint": false,
      "readOnlyHint": false,
    },
    "description": "Takes in-game screenshots of parts of a map, one frame per Shot. A Shot is a rectangle of the map in cells, rect: [left, bottom, right, top], that its frame shows whole, with an optional pitch (degrees down from level: 75 by default, a high view; 56 is the player's camera) and yaw (degrees turned from the game camera's own, which looks north; 0 by default), and a label that names its frame. Use it to see how a map looks in the game itself (ground, cliffs, dressing, water, lighting, a layout's sight lines), not in a preview render or the Editor. Size each rectangle to what you want to judge: the camera moves back until the rectangle fits, so about 10-20 cells shows detail, 30-50 cells a Zone, and more an overview; the 16:9 frame shows more than the rectangle on its long side. A rectangle too big for the farthest camera, or at the map's edge where the camera can't centre on it, is still captured and listed as partial (whole: false). Send every Shot you need in one call: starting the game takes minutes, each Shot after that a few seconds. The game runs a capture copy of the map with none of the map's own triggers: the map as placed, revealed with no fog, no UI, no trigger-set lighting (pass lighting, a Light id, to set one), no trigger-spawned units and no trigger-made actor changes. Give mission for a Mission's own Map Package (frames in missions/<id>/output/shots/<name>/, git-ignored) or mapPath for any Map Package (frames in the machine's cacheDir, shots/<name>/); name is the folder, the time by default. Every capture on the machine goes through one Editor Broker, one at a time, so a call may wait behind others. It waits up to 10 minutes; a job not done by then comes back as state waiting with a job id: call capture_shots({ job }) to wait again and collect the frames, or hear the job's place in the queue. capture_shots({ job, cancel: true }) cancels a job no longer wanted, waiting or running (a running one quits the game and closes its copy first), and stopping a call cancels the job it waits on. Returns the frames' paths and shots.json, which gives each Shot's rectangle, camera and whether its frame shows it whole; look at the frames with your file-reading tool. Needs the macOS Host Adapter (editor.captureTour) and an awake screen; the Editor comes to the front while it runs, so check the author isn't mid-task in it.",
    "execution": {
      "taskSupport": "forbidden",
    },
    "inputSchema": {
      "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
      "properties": {
        "cancel": {
          "description": "With job: cancels it, whether it waits in the queue or runs",
          "type": "boolean",
        },
        "job": {
          "description": "A job id an earlier call returned: waits again, then collects its frames or says where it stands. Give it alone, or with cancel",
          "minLength": 1,
          "type": "string",
        },
        "lighting": {
          "description": "A Light id to set, since the map's own lighting triggers don't run; the tileset's default lighting otherwise",
          "minLength": 1,
          "type": "string",
        },
        "mapPath": {
          "description": "Any Map Package, instead of a Mission: an absolute path, or one relative to the Workspace's root",
          "minLength": 1,
          "type": "string",
        },
        "mission": {
          "description": "The Mission's folder name in the Workspace's missions folder",
          "minLength": 1,
          "type": "string",
        },
        "name": {
          "description": "The frames' folder name: letters, digits, dots, dashes and underscores; the time by default",
          "minLength": 1,
          "type": "string",
        },
        "shots": {
          "description": "The Shots, all of them in one call; at most 99",
          "items": {
            "additionalProperties": false,
            "properties": {
              "label": {
                "minLength": 1,
                "type": "string",
              },
              "pitch": {
                "exclusiveMinimum": 13.89975,
                "maximum": 90,
                "type": "number",
              },
              "rect": {
                "additionalItems": false,
                "description": "[left, bottom, right, top], in cells",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "type": "number",
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "number",
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "number",
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "number",
                  },
                ],
                "maxItems": 4,
                "minItems": 4,
                "type": "array",
              },
              "yaw": {
                "maximum": 360,
                "minimum": -360,
                "type": "number",
              },
            },
            "required": [
              "rect",
            ],
            "type": "object",
          },
          "minItems": 1,
          "type": "array",
        },
        "workspace": {
          "description": "The Workspace this call is about, when it isn't the one the session is in: its folder, a git worktree of it, or any folder inside either (such as a Mission's), absolute. The nearest folder at or above it that holds the Workspace config serves, so a Mission that exists only in a worktree is read and written there, not in the main checkout. Without it the session's working folder serves (the MCP roots), else the Workspace the server started with",
