    },
    "description": "Writes Kept entries of one GameData file of a Workspace mod into its Mod Blueprint as generic typed catalog entries (ADR-0011): each { catalog, class, id, parent, fields }, keeping its imported id, in entries/<file name>.json in the mod's folder, which the Blueprint's entries lists. Fields are written as Blizzard's catalogs write them: a field given as a number or string is its value attribute; an object is a structure whose numbers and strings are its attributes and whose objects and arrays are its own fields; an array is the field once per item. A comment inside an entry is kept where it stands: a $comment key among a structure's fields ($comment2 … for more), or an item holding only $comment among an array's. A class's default is the one entry with no id, and gives attributes { default: "1" }. Each entry takes the localized strings keyed to it with it, by strings file, from the package's Kept strings or the Blueprint's raw strings (which they leave): those its catalog's convention names for its id (<Catalog>/<Field>/<id>, such as Unit/Name/<id>, Button/Tooltip/<id>, Button/Hotkey/<id>, */EditorPrefix/<id>) and those its fields name (UserData text, a ConversationState's names) unless the convention gives them to another entry of the mod; so does each entry of the file the Blueprint already writes, a generic one or a declared character's CCharacter, so running it again gives earlier conversions their strings. An entry stays Kept, with the reason, when its class isn't in the game's data schema, it doesn't fit the schema (an unknown field, a wrong kind of value), its generic form wouldn't read back the same (a comment included) or the file has its key twice. Nothing in the package changes: compile_mod then writes the entries over their Kept originals under the Equivalence Check, moving them from Kept to Compiled.",
    "execution": {
      "taskSupport": "forbidden",
    },
