    },
    "description": "Writes the sound set of one of a Workspace Campaign Mod's Screenplay conversations into its Mod Blueprint's soundSets, and the conversation's voices into the Blueprint of the Asset Mod it depends on that holds the voice files (ADR-0011), from each line's Kept CSound and voice file. A CSound with a Template is written in the template form (on its parent, whose AssetArrayTemplate File gives the file), one with its AssetArray in full in the AssetArray form; the most common form is the set's, and a line in another has its own (lines). Each line's voice file becomes its recording: the Asset Mod's own assets declaration of the file, moved into voices, else the package's file copied to voices/<Sound>.<format> in the Asset Mod's folder with its SHA-256. A voice file that is a Git LFS pointer is recorded by the SHA-256 it points to, so compile_mod holds the package's file until the recording is pulled and never writes over a voice without it. A line with no voice file gets a placeholder until it's recorded. A line CSound convert_entries wrote as a Generic Entry moves into the sound set. Each line CSound's name (Sound/Name/<id> in ObjectStrings) is the sound set's, which writes its id: it leaves the Blueprint's raw strings and a moved CSound's strings, and a Kept name that isn't the id is listed as a difference. A CSound no sound set can write refuses the conversation, naming each, and nothing is written. What the compile will find different from the Kept CSounds (such as a renumbered line's Line) is listed, to name in acceptedDifferences. Nothing in either package changes: compile_mod on the Asset Mod, then on the Campaign Mod, writes them over their Kept originals under the Equivalence Check.",
    "execution": {
      "taskSupport": "forbidden",
    },
