        "warnings": {
          "items": {
            "type": "string",
          },
          "type": "array",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "changed",
        "warnings",
        "files",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "title": "Edit Kept objects and regions",
  },
  {
    "annotations": {
      "destructiveHint": true,
      "readOnlyHint": false,
    },
    "description": "Edits the heights, cliffs, ramps, textures and painted pathing of the Mission's Kept ground, in its own Map Package (ADR-0010). The area is a Kept region ({ region }: Editor id, Kept Reference id or name), a point and a radius ({ point, radius }: a point's Editor Id, Kept Reference id or name, or { x, y }), a rectangle of cells ({ rect: { x, y, width, height } }) or a polygon ({ polygon }), with a falloff: "soft" (the default, 3 cells), "sharp" or a width in cells. Height brushes: raise / lower { by } in height units (a cliff level is 2), flatten { to: "mean" (the default) | a height | { at: point } }, smooth { passes }. They change the height map's base layer, so cliffs keep their height: only t3HeightMap and t3SyncHeightMap change, vertex by vertex. Cliff brushes: raise-cliff / lower-cliff { cliffSet? } move every corner of the 2-cell cliff grid inside the area one level (no falloff), with new cliffs in the set painted on each cell or the one named from the map's cliff sets. add-ramp { at, uphill, low, high, narrow?, cliffSet? } stamps one of the generator's stock ramps (straight for north/south/east/west: at is the middle of its top edge; diagonal: at is the corner it cuts), refused unless the ground there is the generator's edge from low to high = low + 1. remove-ramp { at } takes out the stock ramp nearest a point. A ramp brush takes no area: its footprint is the area. Cliff and ramp edits also write t3SyncCliffLevel, t3CellFlags and t3Terrain.xml, and reset t3VertCol's static shadow bake: the author then rebakes, Data > Generate Static Shadows (Cmd-Shift-L) in the Editor. Paint brushes: paint { texture } paints the area with one of the map's textures (its name, or its slot: 0-7 in set 0, 8s to 8s+7 in texture set s), feathered in t3TextureMasks over the falloff, and gives each changed cell its dominant texture in t3SyncTextureInfo; a texture the map lacks is refused, naming its textures. On a map with several texture sets (one per 8×8-cell block) a stroke paints only blocks whose set holds the texture, leaving 2 texels each side of a boundary between sets, as the Editor does, and names the blocks it left. swap-slot { slot, texture } (no area) puts another texture in a slot map-wide, changing the look of every cell painted with it (at most 16 different textures a map): without confirm it only renders the cells the slot covers, map-wide (area.png), for the author to see; with confirm: true, after the author's yes, it writes t3Terrain.xml's texture list and t3SyncTextureInfo's names. set-blocks { set } (index or name) moves the 8×8-cell blocks the area touches onto another texture set, as the Editor's Texture Set brush: each texel keeps its weights, so its textures swap slot for slot. Without confirm it only renders the blocks before and after; with confirm: true it writes their tileSet in t3Terrain.xml and their cells' set in t3SyncTextureInfo. clear-slot { slot } (no area) empties a slot (its number, or the name of the texture in it) to make room under the 16, writing its name as "" in t3Terrain.xml's texture list and t3SyncTextureInfo's names, as the Editor writes an empty slot; it is refused, naming the cells, while any cell of the slot's set has weight on its layer or has it as its sync texture, so it changes no cell's look and needs no confirm, and refused, naming the Zones, while a generating Zone the next compile paints from that set names its texture. A slot of a texture set of the Blueprint's (textureSets) is Compiled Content, for swap-slot and clear-slot alike: change the set in the Blueprint instead. The other paint brushes warn where a texture touches itself across a boundary between sets, which the Editor renders with glitches. Pathing brushes: block-pathing paints the area's cells unpathable, and clear-pathing clears their paint so they're pathable again, in CellAttribute_Pnp (the Editor's pathing brush layer), whole cells with no falloff (leave falloff out, or "sharp"). Only the paint changes: cliffs and doodad footprints still block. Objects changes when Kept doodads on the changed cells move with the ground (leaveDoodads leaves them). Writes an area render (confirm it shows the right ground), and before, after and difference renders, to missions/<id>/renders/<name>/; clear-slot, which changes no cell's look, renders nothing. dryRun renders and reports without writing the package: use it to confirm the area first. Refused on cells the compile record lists (naming the Blueprint field) and while the Editor has the map open. To look at the edits in game, when the author asks or after a batch, run \`npm run capture-edit -- <blueprint.json> <edit.json>...\` in sc2-adjutant: a camera tour of each area on a copy, frames in the research archive (it takes minutes, and needs the Host Adapter's editor.captureTour).",
    "execution": {
      "taskSupport": "forbidden",
    },
